From 738b659473ba9bdc1cac0947365dc66c4e39fabf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 19:53:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 40/44] aout: correct drift by time scaling instead of
 resampling

Resampling corrects drift by playing the stream at a different rate,
which shifts pitch along with speed, and it does so through whichever
resampler the build has - where libsamplerate is absent that is "ugly",
which drops and duplicates samples. Correcting half a percent of drift
that way left a 440 Hz tone 7.5 cents sharp with a residual 17% of the
signal level off-tone, continuously, for as long as it was corrected.

Drive the rate filter instead. That is scaletempo where time stretching
is enabled, which varies speed without touching pitch: the same tone
comes out at 440.00 Hz with the residual at 0.2%.

The bound is then a range of playback speeds rather than an interval of
detune, so "aout-max-resampling" gives way to "aout-timescale-min" and
"aout-timescale-max". A change of speed is far less noticeable than the
detuning it replaces, so the range can be wide - it is there to stop a
runaway, not to keep the correction quiet. Without a time stretcher the
drift now goes uncorrected rather than being detuned away.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
